## The idea

Use a **sliding window** with two pointers. Count how many characters of t are still **missing** from the window. Move the right pointer to grow the window until nothing is missing; then move the left pointer to shrink it as long as it stays complete, recording the smallest window seen. Each pointer only moves forward, so the whole scan is linear. For the circular version, scan the string written twice (s + s), but ignore windows longer than s.

## Step by step

1. Count the characters of t in a dictionary `need` (how many of each we still need). `missing` = length of t.
2. Move `right` across s. If s[right] is still needed (count > 0), `missing` goes down. Always decrease need[s[right]].
3. While `missing` is 0 the window is complete: record it if it is the smallest so far, then remove s[left] — if that makes a needed count positive, `missing` goes up — and move `left` forward.
4. At the end, return the smallest window, or "" if none was complete.
5. Circular: run the same scan on s + s, only accepting windows of length ≤ |s| that start inside the first copy.

## Worked example

**Input:** s = "ADOBECODEBANC", t = "ABC"

| Step | What happens |
|---|---|
| right → 5 | window "ADOBEC" has A, B, C → complete; best = "ADOBEC" (6) |
| shrink | remove A → missing A → stop shrinking; left = 1 |
| right → 10 | window "DOBECODEBA" complete again (got another A) |
| shrink | drop D, O, B, E, C … until a needed letter is lost; best stays 6 |
| right → 12 | window "BANC" complete → length 4 → new best |
| end | no smaller complete window |

**Result:** "BANC"

## Cost

O(|s| + |t|) time: each pointer moves at most |s| steps. O(number of distinct characters) memory. Circular: O(2|s|).

## The code (C#, compiled and tested against every part of the question)

```csharp
public static class MinWindow {

    public static string Find(string s, string t) {
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(s) || string.IsNullOrEmpty(t)) return "";
        int[] best = Scan(s, t, s.Length, s.Length);
        if (best[1] == 0) return "";
        return s.Substring(best[0], best[1]);
    }

    // The window may run off the end of s and continue at the start.
    public static string FindCircular(string s, string t) {
        if (string.IsNullOrEmpty(s) || string.IsNullOrEmpty(t)) return "";
        string doubled = s + s;
        // only windows that start in the first copy and are no longer than s are real
        int[] best = Scan(doubled, t, s.Length, s.Length);
        if (best[1] == 0) return "";
        return doubled.Substring(best[0], best[1]);
    }

    // Returns { start, length } of the smallest complete window (length 0 = none found).
    private static int[] Scan(string s, string t, int maxStart, int maxLength) {
        Dictionary<char, int> need = new Dictionary<char, int>();
        foreach (char ch in t) {
            if (need.ContainsKey(ch)) need[ch]++;
            else need[ch] = 1;
        }

        int missing = t.Length;      // characters of t not yet covered by the window
        int left = 0;
        int bestStart = 0, bestLength = 0;

        for (int right = 0; right < s.Length; right++) {
            char add = s[right];
            if (need.ContainsKey(add)) {
                if (need[add] > 0) missing--;       // this character was still needed
                need[add]--;                        // may go negative: we have extras
            }

            while (missing == 0) {                  // window [left..right] is complete
                int length = right - left + 1;
                bool better = bestLength == 0 || length < bestLength;
                if (better && length <= maxLength && left < maxStart) {
                    bestStart = left;
                    bestLength = length;
                }
                char drop = s[left];                // try to shrink from the left
                if (need.ContainsKey(drop)) {
                    need[drop]++;
                    if (need[drop] > 0) missing++;  // we just lost a needed character
                }
                left++;
            }
        }
        return new int[] { bestStart, bestLength };
    }
}
